{% if header_delivery_mode is defined and header_delivery_mode == "structured_redirect" %}
// Laravel's redirect()->away() helper: a structured, framework-owned
// redirect response, never a raw string-concatenated header line. The
// value was already validated above (no control characters, a
// same-origin relative path only), so away() -- which normally accepts
// any absolute URL -- is safe to use here.
return redirect()->away({{ value_expr }});
{% else %}
// Raw, unstructured header construction -- the classic response-
// splitting footgun this family models (CWE-113). PHP's own header()
// function has unconditionally rejected an embedded CR/LF in a single
// call since PHP 5.1.2 (re-verified directly against a real PHP
// interpreter, both bare CLI and a real `php -S` built-in server, during
// CC-LAB-0218's pre-change review) -- so this literal call is not
// independently exploitable through this framework's own SAPI. The
// safety matrix's own no_effect verdict on this op still holds: it
// records that the application applies no defense of its own, not a
// claim that every possible delivery path for this exact code is
// exploitable end to end. See CC-LAB-0218's change-control entry and
// tests/test_labgen_header_injection_circlefeed_live_boot.py for the
// raw-socket-level proof of this same raw-concatenation construction's
// real behavior, at the one layer (a hand-rolled response writer, not a
// PHP SAPI) where PHP genuinely applies no such protection.
header("Location: " . {{ value_expr }});
exit;
{% endif %}
